# Shift Brain Dump

Convert an EM physician’s raw end-of-shift brain dump into a structured, actionable sign-out
note — fast, accurate, ready to read aloud or paste into Epic.

-----

## When to Use

- User says “brain dump”, “sign out”, “end of shift”, “/dump”, “/signout”
- User pastes a list of patients in any unstructured format
- User dictates or types a stream of consciousness mid-shift update
- Proactive: if user lists 3+ patients with dispositions, offer: “Want me to format that as a sign-out?”

-----

## Inputs Needed

|Input                   |Required?|How to provide                                   |
|------------------------|---------|-------------------------------------------------|
|Raw patient list or dump|Yes      |Paste or dictate freely — no format needed       |
|Output format preference|Optional |Default: structured table + narrative per patient|
|Pending items flag      |Optional |Any labs, imaging, consults still outstanding    |

-----

## Steps

1. **Parse the dump** — extract each patient, their chief complaint, workup status, disposition, and any pending items. Do not ask clarifying questions first — work with what’s given.
1. **Identify pending items** — flag anything unresolved: labs, imaging, consults, family conversations, disposition decisions.
1. **Flag high-risk patients** — any patient with an unstable or uncertain trajectory gets a ⚠️ marker.
1. **Format the output** — structure as sign-out table + one-liner per patient (see format below).
1. **End with a “Loose Ends” section** — anything that needs action before the next provider takes over.

-----

## Output Format

### Sign-Out Table

|Bed|Name/MRN|CC               |Workup              |Dispo                       |Pending                  |Risk            |
|---|--------|-----------------|--------------------|----------------------------|-------------------------|----------------|
|[#]|[Pt ID] |[chief complaint]|[done / in progress]|[admit / DC / obs / pending]|[labs, imaging, consults]|[⚠️ if high risk]|

### Per-Patient One-Liner

```
Bed [#]: [Age][sex] with [CC]. [Key findings]. [Workup status]. [Plan/dispo]. [Pending: X].
```

### Loose Ends

- [ ] [Action item 1 — who needs to do it]
- [ ] [Action item 2]

-----

## Voice + Constraints

- **Terse.** EM sign-out is not a presentation — it’s a safety transfer. Every word earns its place.
- **No hedging.** Don’t say “it appears” — say what it is.
- **Flag uncertainty explicitly.** “Unclear dispo” or “awaiting attending input” is better than a guess.
- **Preserve Andre’s shorthand** — if he writes “r/o ACS, trop pending x2”, keep that language.
- **Never invent clinical details.** If something is ambiguous, flag it with [?] rather than filling in.

-----

## Example

**Input:**

```
bed 4 chest pain guy, EKG fine, first trop negative, second pending, likely dc home
bed 7 belly pain woman, CT done showing appendicitis, surgery aware, going to OR
bed 12 altered old man, family at bedside, UTI on UA, starting abx, social work involved, 
probably admit medicine but no bed yet
```

**Output:**

|Bed|CC            |Workup               |Dispo            |Pending          |Risk|
|---|--------------|---------------------|-----------------|-----------------|----|
|4  |Chest pain    |EKG ✓, trop x1 neg   |DC home (pending)|Trop x2          |—   |
|7  |Abdominal pain|CT → appendicitis    |OR               |Surgery accepting|—   |
|12 |Altered MS    |UA → UTI, abx started|Admit medicine   |Bed placement, SW|⚠️   |

**One-liners:**

- Bed 4: Middle-aged M with chest pain. EKG normal, trop x1 negative. Trop x2 pending. Likely DC home if negative.
- Bed 7: F with acute abdominal pain. CT confirmed appendicitis. Surgery aware, going to OR.
- Bed 12: Elderly M with altered mental status. UA positive, antibiotics started. Medicine admit pending bed. Social work involved. ⚠️ Family at bedside — needs update before transfer.

**Loose Ends:**

- [ ] Bed 4: Confirm trop x2 result before DC
- [ ] Bed 12: Medicine bed placement — follow up with charge nurse

-----

## Quality Check

Before delivering:

- [ ] Every patient accounted for?
- [ ] All pending items captured?
- [ ] High-risk patients flagged?
- [ ] Loose ends actionable — not vague?
- [ ] Would the oncoming provider know exactly what to do with each patient?
